>> title: Siu Yuk
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: pork, chinese, roast, crackling, oven
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: siu yook, sio bak, 燒肉, roast pork belly, crispy skin pork, crispy skin roast pork
>> servings: 6
>> time: 15 hr
>> slack: unforgiving — a skin that goes into the oven damp steams and stays leather, and no blast at 465°F pops it afterwards

>> step: blanch 5 min, dry the skin hard
Blanch @pork belly{2 1/2%lb}(1.15 kg; skin on, one thick slab) skin-down in a #pot{} of boiling water for ~blanch{5%min}, then lift it out and dry the skin with a cloth until it squeaks.

>> step: rub the cut faces only, keep the skin dry
Rub @kosher salt{2%tsp}, @sugar{2%tsp}, @Chinese five-spice powder{1%tsp}, @white pepper{1/2%tsp} and @Shaoxing wine{1%Tbs} into the meat and the cut sides of @&(~1)belly{}, keeping every grain off the skin — seasoning up there pulls water back into it.

>> step: salt the skin, prick it all over, air-dry uncovered 12 hr
Prick the skin of @&(~1)seasoned belly{} all over with a #skewer{} until it is stippled like a thimble, rub @coarse sea salt{1%tsp} across it, and stand it uncovered on a rack in the fridge to ~dry{12%hr}.

>> step: roast 325°F (160°C) 1 hr 30 min
Roast @&(~1)belly{} skin-up on a rack over a foil-lined #sheet pan{} in a 325°F (160°C) #oven{} for ~roast{1 1/2%hr}, until the meat is tender and the skin is dull and tight.

>> step: roast 465°F (240°C) 25 min, rest 15 min, chop through the crackling
Turn the #oven{} up to 465°F (240°C) and blast @&(~1)pork{} ~roast{25%min}, until the skin has blistered right across, then ~rest{15%min} and chop it into 1-in squares.
